  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSouth to southwest H5 flow prevails today, with surface winds out of \nthe south to southeast. Showers and isolated thunderstorms are \nexpected overnight as a shortwave moves along the flow pattern. Most \nareas should see wetting rains, with the heavier amounts being in \nwest central Minnesota and southeast North Dakota. Heading into \nThursday, H5 flow becomes northwesterly, with surface flow remaining \nout of the south to southeast. Weak shortwave ridging will support \nslightly warmer high temperatures in the low 70s on Friday. Heading \ninto Saturday, a trough is expected to traverse southwest flow into \nthe Northern Plains, bringing another chance for rain into the \nregion. Ensembles are in reasonably good agreement regarding \nadditional rain chances late Sunday through Tuesday in association \nwith a cold front. At this time, this system carries the potential \nfor much higher rainfall totals over this period, with currently a \n25 percent chance for 1 inch or more rain across much of eastern \nNorth Dakota and northwest Minnesota.",
